# Line endings, and why this file is not cosmetic.
#
# `scripts/db.mjs` records a SHA-256 of each migration's *text* in the
# `schema_migrations` ledger, and refuses to migrate a database whose recorded
# checksum disagrees with the file on disk. That guard exists to catch somebody
# editing an applied migration, which is a real and expensive mistake.
#
# Without this file it also catches something that is not a mistake at all. Git
# on Windows checks out with CRLF by default, and the same migration hashes
# differently from its LF form on Linux:
#
#   0001_initial.sql   LF   bf1403fd0f631670
#                      CRLF b59616db8f49f38f
#
# So a database migrated from a laptop and then touched from CI — or from
# Vercel, or from a second contributor — is refused on a difference nobody made.
# The failure arrives late, at a deploy, reading as schema drift.
#
# Normalising to LF in the repository *and* on checkout makes the checksum a
# property of the migration rather than of the machine that read it. Node,
# Postgres and every editor this project needs read LF on Windows perfectly
# well; nothing here depends on CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one file in the tree that is bytes rather than text.
*.ico binary
